This documentation outlines how to take Drosophila velocity data recorded with the ethoscope device and process it with HCTSA or Catch-22 pipeline to get a 
confusion matrix, classifying accuracy between groups.
The coccinella pipeline demonstrates a cheap and simple way to take unbiased velocity time-series data from the ethoscope device and uses the 
statistical classification tool, HCTSA, or its sister software, Catch-22, to classify groups of flies. 
HCTSA pipeline
•	To access your ethoscope data in R, you will initially have made a metadata (csv) file, which contains all the information about your experiment. 
As explained in the rethomics tutorial, this file must contain three columns of information: the date the ethoscope was started, the machine names 
(ids of ethoscopes used) and a column listing each region of interest. Other columns may be added that denote other variables in your experiment, 
such as the genotype of the fly or a treatment/compound it is exposed to. 
•	Once you have generated your metadata file, you can now use the R script, which utilises the rethomics packages, to firstly download your ethoscope 
files from your server and link the data to the metadata file. This script also allows the generation of single time-series of maximum velocity for each 
individual fly, saved as a single csv file. 
•	After the csv files have been generated, you can download the csv files and use the custom python script to remove index and id columns, leaving a 
single numerical column of velocity time series for each fly, saved in dat file format. 
•	In matlab, these single files need to be associated with keywords. For this, a txt file needs to be made which gives the name of each .dat time-series 
file which will be run through HCTSA and the keywords(s) associated with it. The txt file can then be run in HCTSA and processed. 
